WiFan}
\par \pard\plain \s23\rtlch\af8\afs36 \ltrch\hich\af5\loch\qc\sb60\sa120\keepn\f5\fs36\dbch\af6\ltrpar{\loch
An ESP32 Smart Controller for 3-speed Whole House/Attic Fans}
\par \pard\plain \s18\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\ql\ltrpar\loch

\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af8\afs36\ab \ltrch\hich\af5\loch\ilvl0\outlinelevel0\sb240\sa120\keepn\f5\fs36\b\dbch\af6{\listtext\pard\plain \tab}\ls1 \fi0\li0\lin0\ql\fi0\li0\lin0\ltrpar{\loch
Purpose}
\par \pard\plain \s18\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\ql\ltrpar{\loch
The controller options for many whole house/attic fans are extremely poor/limited. At }{\rtlch\ai \ltrch\loch\i\loch
best}{\rtlch\ai0 \ltrch\loch\i0\loch
, the only one I could find was limited to Bluetooth instead of a semi-proprietary sub-Gigahertz range RF controller. What\u8217\'92s worse is buying a second or replacement controller runs $100 or more and still requires you to climb up into the attic to press a pairing button to resync/pair the controller. Definitely not ideal given how dead simple these fans really are. That\u8217\'92s when it struck me. If I can\u8217\'92t buy a solution, then we\u8217\'92ll build one! }
\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af8\afs36\ab \ltrch\hich\af5\loch\ilvl0\outlinelevel0\sb240\sa120\keepn\f5\fs36\b\dbch\af6{\listtext\pard\plain \tab}\ls1 \fi0\li0\lin0\ql\fi0\li0\lin0\ltrpar{\loch
Requirements}{\loch
 }
\par \pard\plain \s18\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af4 \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\f4\dbch\af4 \u8226\'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls2 \fi-360\li720\lin720\ql\ltrpar{\rtlch\ai0 \ltrch\loch\i0\loch
WiFi \u8211\'96 This is a }{\rtlch\ai0\ab \ltrch\loch\i0\b\loch
MUST}{\rtlch\ai0\ab0 \ltrch\loch\i0\b0\loch
. No proprietary wireless controllers/frequencies/Zigbee bullshit, etc.}
\par \pard\plain \s18\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af4 \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\f4\dbch\af4 \u8226\'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls2 \fi-360\li720\lin720\ql\ltrpar{\rtlch\ai0\ab0 \ltrch\loch\i0\b0\loch
Webpage Support \u8211\'96 Allow for controlling/configuring directly without forcing \u8220\'93Smart\u8221\'94 methods (like apps, Amazon Echo/Apple HomeKit/Google Home devices, etc.)}
\par \pard\plain \s18\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af4 \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\f4\dbch\af4 \u8226\'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls2 \fi-360\li720\lin720\ql\ltrpar{\rtlch\ai0\ab0 \ltrch\loch\i0\b0\loch
Wireless Provisioning Method \u8211\'96 \u8220\'93Install, turn on, get out.\u8221\'94 I don\u8217\'92t want there to be any reason why you have to climb back into the attic outside catastrophic failure. So all setup/configuration and resetting has to be done through the web interface}
\par \pard\plain \s18\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af4 \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\f4\dbch\af4 \u8226\'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls2 \fi-360\li720\lin720\ql\ltrpar{\rtlch\ai0\ab0 \ltrch\loch\i0\b0\loch
Maker Friendly \u8211\'96 Custom/bespoke PCBs are great, but having to get 5 PCBs and convince 4 of your friends to buy one also for something that is a \u8220\'93build/use just one\u8221\'94 controller is not worth it IMHO. Thus the need for designing it to be easy to buy/assemble just 1.}
\par \pard\plain \s18\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140{\listtext\pard\plain \rtlch\af4 \ltrch\hich\af4\loch\f4\dbch\af4 \u8226\'95\tab}\ilvl0\ls2 \fi-360\li720\lin720\ql\ltrpar{\rtlch\ai0\ab0 \ltrch\loch\i0\b0\loch
Source Part Model Agnostic \u8211\'96 Where ever possible, parts/components need to be generic enough that it isn\u8217\'92t difficult to source a replacement from any manufacturer to serve the same purpose.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af8\afs36\ab \ltrch\hich\af5\loch\ilvl0\outlinelevel0\sb240\sa120\keepn\f5\fs36\b\dbch\af6{\listtext\pard\plain \tab}\ls1 \fi0\li0\lin0\ql\fi0\li0\lin0\ltrpar{\loch
Design}
\par \pard\plain \s18\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\ql\ltrpar{\loch
The heart of WiFan is an ESP-32D, AKA the ESP-WROOM-32. It supports 2.4Ghz WiFi, has 4MB of flash, is low cost and supports enough of the Matter library set for Smart home Integration. }{\loch
I\u8217\'92m using this one from Amazon: }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0DF2YJSHN" }{\fldrslt {\loch\loch\cf9\ul\ulc0\loch
https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0DF2YJSHN}}}}
\par \pard\plain \s18\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\ql\ltrpar{\rtlch\ai0 \ltrch\loch\i0\loch
Along with this are 4 normally open 30A high power relays with low level triggers. The fan\u8217\'92s speed is controlled by connecting the incoming HOT/Line AC wire to the appropriate speed wire. So the relays are needed to to connect HOT/Line AC (when triggered) to the appropriate wire on the fan motor. }{\rtlch\ai0 \ltrch\loch\i0\loch
Why 4 relays if we\u8217\'92re only controlling 3 speeds? The 4}{\rtlch\ai0 \ltrch\loch\super\i0\loch
th}{\rtlch\ai0 \ltrch\loch\i0\loch
 is actually wired to a simple latching circuit to kill the 5v power and allow a full power cycle of the ESP32 rather than just a code reboot.}
\par \pard\plain \s18\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\ql\ltrpar{\rtlch\ai0 \ltrch\loch\i0\loch
In addition, it\u8217\'92ll need a 5v DC power supply, and if the relays are not able to be triggered with 3.3v, then a 5v/3.3v level shifter between the relay actuator and ESP32 GPIO lines.}
\par \pard\plain \s18\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\ql\ltrpar{\rtlch\ai0 \ltrch\loch\i0\loch
Fortunately, I found a dual relay module (on Amazon) that not only fits the power requirements but also provides 5v DC with AC input: }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B077W1NVLM" }{\fldrslt {\rtlch\ai0 \ltrch\loch\loch\cf9\ul\ulc0\i0\loch
https://www.amazon.com/dp/B077W1NVLM}{}}}\rtlch\ai0 \ltrch\loch\i0\loch
. Two of these modules provide us with relays we need and redundant power for the ESP32.}
\par \pard\plain \s18\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\ql\ltrpar{\rtlch\ai0 \ltrch\loch\i0\loch
Since ESP32\u8217\'92s support micro-Python and Arduino C code, I used the default Arduino C code and the Matter library for home automation support. This allows for WiFan to be detected and controlled as 3 different \u8220\'93Smart Plugs\u8221\'94 for High, Medium, and Low speeds.}
\par \pard\plain \s1\rtlch\af8\afs36\ab \ltrch\hich\af5\loch\ilvl0\outlinelevel0\sb240\sa120\keepn\f5\fs36\b\dbch\af6{\listtext\pard\plain \tab}\ls1 \fi0\li0\lin0\ql\fi0\li0\lin0\ltrpar{\loch
